1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,277
One sentence from 1964 clears more

2
00:00:02,277 --> 00:00:04,554
dead weight from your calendar and

3
00:00:04,554 --> 00:00:06,965
portfolio in twenty minutes than any

4
00:00:06,965 --> 00:00:08,840
productivity app ever built.

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:11,586
In his 1964 book Managing for Results,

6
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:15,489
Peter Drucker taught executives systematic abandonment

7
00:00:15,489 --> 00:00:16,717
through one test:

8
00:00:16,717 --> 00:00:18,596
'If we were not already in

9
00:00:18,596 --> 00:00:19,391
this today,

10
00:00:19,391 --> 00:00:21,198
knowing what we now know,

11
00:00:21,198 --> 00:00:22,860
would we enter it now?'

12
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:24,437
Without scheduled abandonment,

13
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:26,014
yesterday's lukewarm meetings,

14
00:00:26,014 --> 00:00:27,066
stale side projects,

15
00:00:27,066 --> 00:00:29,326
and forgotten subscriptions quietly eat the

16
00:00:29,326 --> 00:00:31,850
margin needed for tomorrow's best opportunities.

17
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,472
Run Drucker's question every six months

18
00:00:34,472 --> 00:00:35,883
across your calendar,

19
00:00:35,883 --> 00:00:36,286
bills,

20
00:00:36,286 --> 00:00:37,160
and holdings:

21
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:39,378
wherever the honest answer is no,

22
00:00:39,378 --> 00:00:42,000
schedule a clean exit date immediately.

23
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,325
Prune dead commitments twice a year

24
00:00:44,325 --> 00:00:46,318
so your capital and focus stay

25
00:00:46,318 --> 00:00:48,510
concentrated on real compounders.
